# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#, no-wrap
msgid "Propagated noise absorption (PNA)"
msgstr "Absorción de ruido propagado (PNA)"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Title #
#, no-wrap
msgid "Propagated noise absorption (PNA) {#propagated-noise-absorption-pna}"
msgstr "Absorción de ruido propagado (PNA) {#propagated-noise-absorption-pna}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "This package implements propagated noise absorption (PNA) [\\[1\\]](#references) for mitigating errors in the expectation value of observables.\n"
msgstr "Este paquete implementa la absorción de ruido propagado (PNA) [\\[1\\]](#references) para mitigar errores en el valor esperado de observables.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "PNA works by “absorbing” the inverses of the learned noise channels into a target observable using Pauli propagation. That is, each Pauli noise generator in the noise model is classically propagated to the end of the circuit and applied to the observable. This results in a new observable that, when measured on a QPU, mitigates the learned gate noise.\n"
msgstr "PNA funciona «absorbiendo» las inversas de los canales de ruido aprendidos en un observable objetivo mediante propagación de Pauli. Es decir, cada generador de ruido de Pauli del modelo de ruido se propaga clásicamente hasta el final del circuito y se aplica al observable. El resultado es un nuevo observable que, al medirse en una QPU, mitiga el ruido de puertas aprendido.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "This package is suitable for estimating expectation values of general quantum circuits and Pauli observables; however, the technique is generally most effective on circuits that are near-Clifford.\n"
msgstr "Este paquete es adecuado para estimar valores esperados de circuitos cuánticos generales y observables de Pauli; sin embargo, la técnica suele ser más eficaz en circuitos casi Clifford.\n"

